<!DOCTYPE article PUBLIC "-//NLM//DTD Journal Archiving and Interchange DTD v2.3 20070202//EN" "archivearticle.dtd"><article xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" xmlns:mml="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" article-type="letter"><?properties open_access?><front><journal-meta><journal-id journal-id-type="nlm-ta">Environ Health Perspect</journal-id><journal-title>Environmental Health Perspectives</journal-title><issn pub-type="ppub">0091-6765</issn><publisher><publisher-name>National Institute of Environmental Health Sciences</publisher-name></publisher></journal-meta><article-meta><article-id pub-id-type="pmc">1913589</article-id><article-id pub-id-type="doi">10.1289/ehp.10151R</article-id><article-id pub-id-type="publisher-id">ehp0115-a0342a</article-id><article-categories><subj-group subj-group-type="heading"><subject>Perspectives</subject><subj-group><subject>Correspondence</subject></subj-group></subj-group></article-categories><title-group><article-title>Copper Deficiency, Lead, and Paraoxonase: Li et al. Respond</article-title></title-group><contrib-group><contrib contrib-type="author"><name><surname>Li</surname><given-names>Wan-Fen</given-names></name></contrib><aff id="af1-ehp0115-a0342a">Division of Environmental Health and Occupational Medicine, National Health Research Institutes, Zhunan, Taiwan</aff></contrib-group><contrib-group><contrib contrib-type="author"><name><surname>Chuang</surname><given-names>Hung-Yi</given-names></name></contrib><aff id="af2-ehp0115-a0342a">Graduate Institute of Public Health, Kaohsiung Medical University, Kaohsiung, Taiwan, E-mail:<email>ericch@cc.kmu.edu.tw</email></aff></contrib-group><author-notes><fn><p>The authors declare they have no competing financial interests.</p></fn></author-notes><pub-date pub-type="ppub"><month>7</month><year>2007</year></pub-date><volume>115</volume><issue>7</issue><fpage seq="a">A342</fpage><lpage>A342</lpage><copyright-statement>This is an Open Access article: verbatim copying and redistribution of this article are permitted in all media for any purpose, provided this notice is preserved along with the article's original DOI</copyright-statement><copyright-year>2007</copyright-year></article-meta></front><body><p>We appreciate the opportunity to discuss the issue raised by Klevay in his letter. Klevay suggested that the decrease of PON1 activity in lead workers, as we reported in our article (<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="b6-ehp0115-a0342a">Li et al. 2006</xref>), might be due to copper deficiency induced by lead intoxication. Because the scope of our study did not include the interaction between lead and copper, we did not measure plasma copper level, ceruloplasmin, or superoxide dismutase (SOD) activity in our cohort. Therefore, it is not possible to test Klevay&#x02019;s hypothesis at this point.</p><p>Although animal studies have shown that lead ingestion caused a decrease in blood copper level in cattle (<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="b2-ehp0115-a0342a">Doyle and Younger 1984</xref>) and weanling rats (<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="b7-ehp0115-a0342a">Mylroie et al. 1986</xref>), the relationship between lead exposure and copper deficiency in humans is less evident. Although <xref ref-type="bibr" rid="b4-ehp0115-a0342a">Ito et al. (1985)</xref> and <xref ref-type="bibr" rid="b9-ehp0115-a0342a">Patil et al. (2006)</xref> showed a decrease of SOD activity in lead workers, results of other studies did not agree with such findings (el-Gazzar and <xref ref-type="bibr" rid="b3-ehp0115-a0342a">Hamid 1998</xref>; <xref ref-type="bibr" rid="b8-ehp0115-a0342a">Oktem et al. 2004</xref>). Whether long-term lead exposure induces copper deficiency in humans is still in question.</p><p>To our knowledge, there are very few studies dealing with the effects of copper on PON1 activity. <xref ref-type="bibr" rid="b1-ehp0115-a0342a">Debord et al. (2003)</xref> showed that copper ion had weak inhibitory effects on PON1 activity <italic>in vitro</italic>, whereas <xref ref-type="bibr" rid="b5-ehp0115-a0342a">Klevay (2004)</xref> claimed that a copper deficiency diet caused PON1 activity reduction in rats. With little information available, it is difficult to conclude whether copper has any effects on PON1 activity in humans.</p><p>However, because of the link between copper deficiency and cardiovascular disease, we agree with Klevay that in the future, the association between lead exposure, copper deficiency, and serum PON1 activity should be examined.</p></body><back><ref-list><title>References</title><ref id="b1-ehp0115-a0342a"><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Debord</surname><given-names>J</given-names></name><name><surname>Bollinger</surname><given-names>JC</given-names></name><name><surname>Merle</surname><given-names>L</given-names></name><name><surname>Dantoine</surname><given-names>T</given-names></name></person-group><year>2003</year><article-title>Inhibition of human serum arylesterase by metal chlorides</article-title><source>J Inorg Biochem</source><volume>94</volume><issue>1-2</issue><fpage>1</fpage><lpage>4</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="doi">10.1016/S0162-0134(02)00627-X</pub-id><comment>[Online 27 February 2003]</comment><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">12620667</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="b2-ehp0115-a0342a"><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Doyle</surname><given-names>JJ</given-names></name><name><surname>Younger</surname><given-names>RL</given-names></name></person-group><year>1984</year><article-title>Influence of ingested lead on the distribution of lead, iron, zinc, copper and manganese in bovine tissues</article-title><source>Vet Hum Toxicol</source><volume>26</volume><issue>3</issue><fpage>201</fpage><lpage>204</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">6730300</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="b3-ehp0115-a0342a"><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>el-Gazzar</surname><given-names>RM</given-names></name><name><surname>Hamid</surname><given-names>HA</given-names></name></person-group><year>1998</year><article-title>Changes in some metallo-enzymes and trace metals among workers occupationally exposed to lead in battery manufacture</article-title><source>J Egypt Public Health Assoc</source><volume>73</volume><issue>1&#x02013;2</issue><fpage>87</fpage><lpage>96</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">17249213</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="b4-ehp0115-a0342a"><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Ito</surname><given-names>Y</given-names></name><name><surname>Niiya</surname><given-names>Y</given-names></name><name><surname>Kurita</surname><given-names>H</given-names></name><name><surname>Shima</surname><given-names>S</given-names></name><name><surname>Sarai</surname><given-names>S</given-names></name></person-group><year>1985</year><article-title>Serum lipid peroxide level and blood superoxide dismutase activity in workers with occupational exposure to lead</article-title><source>Int Arch Occup Environ Health</source><volume>56</volume><issue>2</issue><fpage>119</fpage><lpage>127</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="doi">10.1007/BF00379383</pub-id><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">4055067</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="b5-ehp0115-a0342a"><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Klevay</surname><given-names>LM</given-names></name></person-group><year>2004</year><article-title>Ischemic heart disease as deficiency disease</article-title><source>Cell Mol Biol (Noisy-le-grand)</source><volume>50</volume><fpage>877</fpage><lpage>884</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">15704251</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="b6-ehp0115-a0342a"><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Li</surname><given-names>WF</given-names></name><name><surname>Pan</surname><given-names>MH</given-names></name><name><surname>Chung</sur